[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWHVPS-30
TITLE: Emission-current grid regulation - Test Case
STATEMENT: >>>
Verify that the firmware adjusts the grid voltage using cathode-current feedback to maintain
the requested emission current during emission.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
* The system is powered on and ready to use.
* A treatment plan is ready to load and grid control is enabled.
* A fixture can control cathode-current feedback above and below the requested current.
* The measured cathode current and grid command can be observed.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Load the treatment plan and record the requested emission current.
2. Start emission and observe the measured current and grid command.
3. Use the fixture to produce current below the target and observe the regulation response.
4. Repeat with current above the target.
5. Allow the current to settle after each disturbance and record the result.
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Verify the requested emission current matches the treatment plan.
2. Verify grid regulation is active during emission.
3. Verify the grid command adjusts in the direction that increases current toward the target.
4. Verify the grid command adjusts in the direction that decreases current toward the target.
5. Verify the measured current returns to and remains within the defined tolerance of the target, with the grid command within configured operating limits.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-14
  ROLE: Verifies

[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWHVPS-31
TITLE: FTDI Bootloader Entry - Test Case
STATEMENT: >>>
A valid FTDI bootloader request shall cause the application firmware to reset into the bootloader.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
* The system is powered on and running the application firmware.
* A USB FTDI host tool that can transmit the seven-byte bootloader frame is connected to USART3.
* An approved method is available to observe the system reset and confirm that the bootloader is active.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Confirm that the application firmware is running and responding normally.
2. Send the seven-byte `*B00TL\n` frame over the FTDI interface.
3. Observe the reset and interrogate the bootloader with the approved host tool.
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Verify the application is operational before the request.
2. Verify the application resets after receiving the valid bootloader frame.
3. Verify the bootloader becomes active and accepts the approved host interaction.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-19
  ROLE: Verifies

[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWHVPS-32
TITLE: FTDI Unknown-Command Rejection and Recovery - Test Case
STATEMENT: >>>
The application shall ignore an unrecognized FTDI command and remain able to process a subsequent
valid command.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
* The system is powered on and running the application firmware.
* A USB FTDI host tool that can transmit and capture binary frames is connected to USART3.
* A valid configuration index and its expected value are available.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Confirm that the application firmware is running and responding normally.
2. Send the unrecognized seven-byte `*B00TX\n` frame over the FTDI interface.
3. Confirm that the application remains operational and that no response is transmitted.
4. Send a valid 11-byte GET frame for the selected configuration index and capture the response.
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Verify the application is operational before testing.
2. Verify the unrecognized command does not reset the system and produces no response.
3. Verify the subsequent GET request returns the expected configuration value, demonstrating that
   FTDI reception recovered for the next valid command.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-19
  ROLE: Verifies

[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWHVPS-38
TITLE: FTDI Configuration GET and SET Commands - Test Case
STATEMENT: >>>
When the system is powered on, the user can retrieve or update an individual configuration
parameter through the FTDI GET and SET protocol. Binary payload bytes do not end a frame early, and
the receiver remains available after traffic arrives while a complete frame awaits processing.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
* The system is powered on and ready to use.
* A USB FTDI host tool that can transmit and capture binary frames is connected to the HVPS board.
* A valid configuration index and an approved value whose little-endian float encoding contains a
  `0x0A` payload byte are available.
* An approved FTDI timing harness can delay deferred command processing while UART receive
  interrupts continue.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Power on the system and begin capturing FTDI traffic.
2. Send an 11-byte GET frame containing `*GET..`, configuration category 0 as a little-endian
   16-bit value, the valid index as a little-endian 16-bit value, and a newline terminator.
3. Record the returned value and response bytes.
4. Send a 15-byte SET frame containing `*..SET`, the same category and index fields, the approved
   value containing the `0x0A` payload byte, and a newline terminator.
5. Record the SET response, then repeat the GET request and record its response.
6. Use the timing harness to deliver one additional byte after a complete valid GET frame while
   deferred processing of that frame remains pending. Release processing, wait for it to complete,
   then send and capture another valid GET request.
7. Send separate requests with an unrecognized command header, an unsupported category, a SET index
   outside the configuration table, and an index of 64 or greater.
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Verify the valid GET returns a 15-byte frame containing `*GET..`, the requested category and
   index, the selected value as a little-endian IEEE-754 32-bit float, and a newline terminator.
2. Verify the `0x0A` payload byte does not terminate the SET frame early. The SET updates the
   selected configuration parameter and returns the corresponding 15-byte `*..SET` confirmation.
3. Verify the repeated GET returns the updated value.
4. Verify the additional byte received while processing is pending is discarded without stalling
   reception, and the final valid GET returns the expected response.
5. Verify each unsupported request is ignored without a response and does not change the selected
   configuration parameter.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-19
  ROLE: Verifies

[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWHVPS-39
TITLE: FTDI Bulk-Read Commands - Test Case
STATEMENT: >>>
When the system is powered on, the user can retrieve every configuration value, setpoint, or
feedback value with the corresponding FTDI bulk-read command.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
* The system is powered on and ready to use.
* A USB FTDI host tool that can transmit and capture binary frames is connected to the HVPS board.
* The expected 32 configuration values and six setpoints are available by index.
* Stable feedback inputs are applied and the expected five feedback values are independently
  observable.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Power on the system and begin capturing FTDI traffic.
2. Send the seven-byte `*ACFGS\n` request and capture the complete response.
3. Send the seven-byte `*ASPTS\n` request and capture the complete response.
4. Record the five stable feedback values, send the seven-byte `*AFBVS\n` request, and capture the
   complete response.
5. Decode the bytes after each response's six-byte `*` and command header as consecutive
   little-endian IEEE-754 32-bit floats through the final newline.
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Verify `ACFGS` produces exactly one 135-byte response beginning with `*ACFGS`, followed by all 32
   configuration values in increasing index order, and ending with a newline.
2. Verify `ASPTS` produces exactly one 31-byte response beginning with `*ASPTS`, followed by all six
   setpoints in increasing index order, and ending with a newline.
3. Verify `AFBVS` produces exactly one 27-byte response beginning with `*AFBVS`, followed by all five
   feedback values in increasing index order, and ending with a newline.
4. Verify every decoded value matches the corresponding expected value for its category and index.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-19
  ROLE: Verifies

[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWHVPS-33
TITLE: Filament-current ramping - Test Case
STATEMENT: >>>
Verify that filament current ramps during warmup, warmup completes when current is stable,
and the filament output stops when a persistent feedback mismatch occurs.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
* The system is powered on and a treatment plan is ready to load.
* A test fixture can control filament-current feedback.
* The requested current, measured current, filament command, and warming status can be observed.
* The configured filament-current limits are available.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Load the treatment plan and observe filament-current ramping during warmup.
2. Hold measured current between the target and 2% above it for 4 seconds.
3. Restart warmup. Once the command reaches the configured minimum, hold measured current
   more than 300 mA below the command for 6 seconds.
4. Request a filament-current target below the configured minimum.
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Verify the filament command increases gradually during warmup and stays within configured limits.
2. Verify warming remains active until the current has been stable for 4 seconds, then clears.
3. Verify the filament command becomes zero and warming clears after the mismatch persists
   for 6 seconds.
4. Verify the requested target and filament command become zero and warming clears.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-6
  ROLE: Verifies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-13
  ROLE: Verifies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-17
  ROLE: Verifies

[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWHVPS-34
TITLE: High voltage setpoint 1 - Test Case
STATEMENT: >>>
When user loads a treatment plan and it is ready for running, make sure the firmware provides and
sets the high voltage setpoint.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
* The system is powered on and ready to run any treatment plan.
* A treatment plan is ready to load into the system.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Power on the system.
2. Load a 20 second treatment plan.
3. Check the state of the system.
4. Check the value of the reported high voltage.
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Verify the system is powered on successfully.
2. Verify the plan is loaded successfully.
3. Verify its state is Ready.
4. Record the value and verify it's within 10% of loaded treatment target.
<<<
